    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if my LDL is high, how does it actually affect my arteries? like what's happening inside them physically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When LDL levels are high, this excess cholesterol can seep into the artery walls, where it triggers an immune response that leads to the formation of fatty deposits called plaques. This process, known as atherosclerosis, narrows the arteries and stiffens them, making it harder for blood to flow and reducing oxygen supply to your heart and other organs. Over time, these plaques can also rupture, causing blood clots that may lead to heart attacks or strokes.",
      "source": "target",
